[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Localizing Templates, Components, and Services in Angular"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Angular provides robust support for internationalization (i18n), allowing you to adapt your application to different languages and regions. This tutorial covers localizing templates, components, and services to create a truly global Angular application."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting up Angular i18n"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before you can localize your application, you need to configure Angular i18n. This involves installing the necessary packages and configuring your project."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Install the '@angular/localize' package: 'npm install @angular/localize'"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enable i18n in your 'angular.json' file. Add the 'localize' option to your build configuration."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":""architect": { 
  "build": { 
    "configurations": { 
      "production": { 
        "localize": true, 
        "browserTarget": "your-app:build:production"
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Localizing Templates"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Templates are localized using the 'i18n' attribute. This attribute marks text for translation."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"<h1 i18n="pageTitle|An introduction title">Hello, World!</h1>"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'i18n' attribute value consists of a meaning (pageTitle) and a description (An introduction title), separated by a pipe (|). The meaning helps translators differentiate between similar text strings."},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also localize attributes using the 'i18n-attribute' syntax."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"<img src="logo.png" alt="Company Logo" i18n-alt="companyLogo|The logo of our company">"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Localizing Components"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Components can be localized by using the '$localize' tagged template literal."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-greeting',
  template: '<div>{{ greeting }}</div>',
})
export class GreetingComponent {
  greeting = $localize`:greeting|A simple greeting message:Hello, there!`
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"The '$localize' tag allows you to mark strings within your component code for translation. The syntax is similar to the 'i18n' attribute, with a meaning and a description."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Localizing Services"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Services can also be localized using the '$localize' tagged template literal. This is useful for localizing messages or data retrieved from a service."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root',
})
export class MessageService {
  getMessage(): string {
    return $localize`:welcomeMessage|A welcome message:Welcome to our application!`;
  }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Extracting Translation Messages"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once you've marked your templates, components, and services for translation, you need to extract the messages into a translation file. Use the following command:"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"ng extract-i18n"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"This command generates an 'messages.xlf' (or 'messages.xml') file containing all the translatable text."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Translating and Building for Different Locales"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Translate the 'messages.xlf' file into the desired languages. Create separate translation files for each locale (e.g., 'messages.fr.xlf' for French)."},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Build your application for each locale using the '--localize' flag and specifying the locale code."},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"ng build --localize fr"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"This command builds a French version of your application. Repeat this process for each supported locale."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Serving Localized Applications"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"To serve a specific locale during development, use the '--configuration' flag with the locale configuration defined in 'angular.json'."},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"ng serve --configuration fr"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"This will serve the French version of your application during development."},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"By following these steps, you can effectively localize your Angular templates, components, and services, creating a truly internationalized application. Remember to thoroughly test your application in each locale to ensure accuracy and a positive user experience."}]
